WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.189
YUFENG GUO: Hi, I'm
here with Chris Law

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:11.970
to learn more about how to
protect highly sensitive data

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.784
from being exfiltrated.

00:00:13.784 --> 00:00:14.700
CHRIS LAW: Hi, Yufeng.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.760
Happy to be here.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.760
YUFENG GUO: So I've
seen a lot of headlines

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.400
over the last year
about big data breaches.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.590
And they seem to be
getting bigger and bigger.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.640
What's going on?

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.400
CHRIS LAW: Yes, any company
storing sensitive data

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.010
needs to be ready to protect
against data breaches.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.530
YUFENG GUO: And so when you
say highly sensitive data, what

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:32.730
do you mean exactly?

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:34.230
CHRIS LAW: Highly
sensitive data can

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.870
be things like social
security numbers, credit card

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.920
numbers, or patient data.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.980
This includes things
like PII, that's

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.140
personally identifiable
information,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.320
financial information, or PHI.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.257
And that's protected
health information.

00:00:47.257 --> 00:00:48.840
But highly sensitive
data could really

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.300
be any data that your company
deems to be sensitive.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.040
But the good news
is Google Cloud

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.540
can help protect this data.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.594
I'll show you how.

00:00:55.594 --> 00:00:57.510
So let's say you're
building your finance app,

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.510
and your partner is sending
you a bunch of data.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.690
The partner is sending you both
sensitive data in red here,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.989
and non-sensitive data in green.

00:01:05.989 --> 00:01:06.780
YUFENG GUO: Got it.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.500
So I really want to keep
that red golf ball secure.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:11.412
CHRIS LAW: Exactly.

00:01:11.412 --> 00:01:13.620
Now the easiest way to get
this data from the partner

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.510
is to create a public
bucket in Google Cloud

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.080
and give them access to it.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.560
So now, as you can
see, the partner

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.210
is sending the sensitive
data and non-sensitive data

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.920
all into this one bucket.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.570
But there's a problem
with that setup.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.047
And it's that
unauthorized parties

00:01:26.047 --> 00:01:27.630
can get access to
these public buckets

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.490
and can exfiltrate the data.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.790
And any exposure of, say, social
security numbers or credit card

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.040
information can put you and
your company in the headlines.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.920
But the good news is
developers like yourself

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.320
can use Google Cloud's
Identity and Access Management

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.400
System, or IAM, to restrict
access to the bucket that

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.030
stores this sensitive data.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.750
YUFENG GUO: So
how does IAM work?

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.610
CHRIS LAW: With
IAM, as a developer,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.830
you can control exactly
which users have access

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.360
to the new restricted bucket.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.210
And each user will have to
securely authenticate in order

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.620
to get any access to data.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.370
YUFENG GUO: Right.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.520
So as a developer, I can
just lock this bucket down

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.770
so only a few people
can access it?

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:05.700
CHRIS LAW: Exactly.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.090
Anyone trying to get access to
data in your restricted bucket

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.520
needs to be authorized
before they can get access

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.420
to any sensitive data.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.570
YUFENG GUO: So I have
this new restricted bucket

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.370
with limited access.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.877
How do I get the social security
number data into that bucket?

00:02:20.877 --> 00:02:21.960
CHRIS LAW: Great question.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.180
Well, let's take a look
at how you can do it.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.220
One way you can sort this
social security number

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.767
data into this restricted
bucket is to do it manually.

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:33.350
The problem with this approach
is that it might make a mistake

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:34.860
and categorize the data wrong.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.930
So in this example,
I might accidentally

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.564
miscategorize the social
security number data

00:02:39.564 --> 00:02:40.980
and put it into
the public bucket,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.340
exposing it to the wrong people.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:44.230
YUFENG GUO: Got it.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.188
CHRIS LAW: For highly
sensitive data like this,

00:02:46.188 --> 00:02:48.810
it makes sense to automate this
sensitive data classification

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.869
process as much as possible.

00:02:50.869 --> 00:02:53.160
And that's what developers
use Google Cloud's Data Loss

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.400
Prevention, or DLP API, for.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.460
DLP can identify over
90 plus different types

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.850
of common sensitive data types
like social security numbers

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.210
and credit card numbers.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:07.650
So with DLP enabled, now when
the partner sends you data,

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.220
DLP will automatically identify
the social security numbers

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.207
and automatically put that data
into the restricted bucket.

00:03:14.207 --> 00:03:15.040
YUFENG GUO: Amazing.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:15.660
CHRIS LAW: Pretty cool.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:16.080
YUFENG GUO: Yeah.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.150
CHRIS LAW: So now that we've
automated this process,

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.733
we've got the sensitive
data all going

00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:22.590
into the restricted bucket,
and only a few select users

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:23.850
have access to it.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:24.600
YUFENG GUO: Great.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.140
So can I launch my app now?

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.140
CHRIS LAW: Not just yet.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.020
This setup works fine as long
as the partner sends the data

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.430
into the right API.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.200
But let's say they
accidentally email

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.510
through a bunch of sensitive
social security number data.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.330
So over here, we have a bunch
of e-mails, white golf balls,

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.990
and e-mails with sensitive
data, which are red golf balls.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.700
Anyone who receives
this email can

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.250
download the social security
number data into to machine

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.050
or forward it into
a personal drive.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.284
This makes it much more
likely to be exposed.

00:03:54.284 --> 00:03:56.700
YUFENG GUO: So how can we keep
this social security number

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.164
data out of inboxes?

00:03:58.164 --> 00:03:59.580
CHRIS LAW: To
handle this problem,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.710
you can use Google Cloud's
Data Loss Prevention

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.010
technology in G Suite.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:08.040
So with G Suite DLP set up,
the social security number data

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.380
within the e-mails gets
automatically detected

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:11.951
and moved into a
quarantine bucket.

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.950
YUFENG GUO: And so now
that social security data

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.030
doesn't hit my inbox.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:15.930
CHRIS LAW: Exactly.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.360
And once the data is moved
into the quarantine bucket,

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.366
admins can review and
handle it more securely.

00:04:21.366 --> 00:04:23.490
YUFENG GUO: So we've got
the social security number

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.960
data going securely into
this restricted bucket.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.950
And now only a few select
users can access it.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:33.660
But what's to stop one
of these legitimate users

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.030
from just stealing the data?

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.830
CHRIS LAW: Right,
great questions.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:39.870
Insider attacks
are also a threat.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.750
So even if you're putting the
data in a secure environment,

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.180
a user who is granted
access to that data

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.040
may have malicious
intent and might

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.900
try to exfiltrate the data.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.570
Leaks like this can be
contained and controlled

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.600
using Google Cloud's
VPC Service Controls.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.140
With VPC Service Controls, we
can set up a secure access zone

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.520
that acts as a perimeter
around the sensitive data

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.990
so even insiders can't
move the data out.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.440
YUFENG GUO: So even a user
I granted legitimate access

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.230
to would not be able to
exfiltrate that data.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:09.500
CHRIS LAW: That's right.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.740
YUFENG GUO: What if an admin
goes in and just changes

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:12.912
these settings?

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:15.370
CHRIS LAW: With Google Cloud's
Forseti open source security

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.450
tools, you can set up an alert.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.960
So when an admin makes any
changes to sensitive data

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.720
settings, an alert can be
sent to Cloud Security Command

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.840
Center so that even admin
access can be monitored.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:26.710
YUFENG GUO: Wow.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:29.339
So are we ready to
launch the app now?

00:05:29.339 --> 00:05:31.630
CHRIS LAW: Well, let's take
a look at your finance app.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:34.180
The partner is sending you
social security number data

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.220
and it's all being categorized
in the right bucket.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:40.050
There's a secure access
zone around the data.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:42.580
And even if the partner
sends social security number

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.240
data via email, that data is
automatically quarantined.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:47.920
I think you're ready
to launch your app.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:48.670
YUFENG GUO: Great.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:50.650
Well, Chris, thanks
for showing me

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.370
how developers and companies can
protect highly sensitive data

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:56.142
like this with Google Cloud.

00:05:56.142 --> 00:05:57.100
CHRIS LAW: My pleasure.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:00.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

